+/* ===== UDP-optimize start ===== */
+/* Cache occupied by the receive rings that are actually being written into.
+ *
+ * A socket's receive buffer competes for cache with the descriptor pages the
+ * NIC is filling, and that term is not small: on mlx5 a 1024-entry ring at a
+ * 9000-byte MTU pins 16MB of an 18MB cache.  A budget taken as a fraction of
+ * the cache alone therefore hands out space that does not exist.
+ *
+ * Two things make this harder than reading a ring size.  The conversion from
+ * entries to bytes belongs to the descriptor format - mlx5's multi-packet WQE
+ * carries many pages per entry, which is why 1024 entries are 16MB and not
+ * 4MB - so only the driver can do it.  And a machine has as many rings as
+ * cores, twenty-four here by default, while a given moment's traffic may be
+ * landing on one of them; the rings that nothing is writing into are not in
+ * cache, and counting them once cost the whole budget and collapsed the
+ * receive buffer to nothing.
